The `digits2` function reads `value * 2` bytes into a string. If `value >= 100`, this would be an out-of-bounds read. But `digits2` is called with `to_unsigned(value) % 100`, so value is always in [0, 100).

Let me look at the `write2` function in `tm_writer`:
```cpp
void write2(int value) {
    const char* d = digits2(to_unsigned(value) % 100);
    *out_++ = *d++;
    *out_++ = *d;
}
```

This is fine since `value % 100` is in [0, 100).

Let me look at the `write_utc_offset` function:
```cpp
void write_utc_offset(long offset) {
    if (offset < 0) {
      *out_++ = '-';
      offset = -offset;
    } else {
      *out_++ = '+';
    }
    offset /= 60;
    write2(static_cast<int>(offset / 60));
    write2(static_cast<int>(offset % 60));
}
```

This is fine too.

